These are **Marvin framework libraries/components used by the application build and runtime**.

The important point is that these versions are interdependent. In particular, **`marvin-core.version` controls the underlying Marvin framework dependency versions**, and when we upgrade `marvin-core.version` to the required secure version, the corresponding **Marvin base components/base image versions may also be updated internally through the framework dependency hierarchy**. Therefore, this is not simply an individual library upgrade; we need to verify the complete Marvin dependency chain and ensure that the compatible base versions are picked up.

The upgrade activity will therefore involve:

* Upgrading `marvin-core.version` to the approved secure version.
* Verifying the corresponding `marvin-spring-boot-base-run` version/base image that is brought in by the upgraded Marvin framework.
* Upgrading `marvin-maven-plugin` where required for compatibility with the new Marvin version.
* Resolving any dependency or compatibility issues introduced by the framework upgrade.
* Performing build validation, unit/integration testing, and regression testing.
* Confirming that the Nexus-reported vulnerabilities are addressed after the upgrade.

Since Marvin is an internal framework built on Spring Boot, the upgrade may also introduce transitive dependency changes. Hence, we need to validate the complete application after the framework upgrade rather than changing only the individually reported vulnerable dependency.

This activity is primarily a **framework/dependency security remediation** for KASH and is required to bring the application onto the supported and secure Marvin versions.

